<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Mawsynram State</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Mawsynram</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Khasi state on the southern edge of the Meghalaya plateau, around the village now often called the wettest inhabited place on Earth.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hima Mawsynram was one of the Khasi chiefdoms of the plateau; its origins are known from oral tradition, and the register's start date is approximate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like other Khasi states it was headed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syiem">Syiem</a> from a royal clan, chosen with clan elders, with succession through the female line. Little written record of its history exists.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Mawsynram came under British supervision in the 19th century and acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1948, joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>. It is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-khasi-hills">East Khasi Hills</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meghalaya">Meghalaya</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1500 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:8.92%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mawsynram</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Khasi traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khasi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hima Mawsynram</dd></dl></aside></div>
